  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=0.\n$$",
